        "content": "The government of India and state governments have established a dedicated network of development finance institutions, regulatory bodies, and promotional agencies to assist rural and agricultural ventures at every lifecycle stage.\n\n### Key Institutional Pillars\n- **NABARD (National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development):** Established in 1982 under Act 61 of 1981 (B. Sivaraman Committee). Functions as the apex development bank providing refinance to commercial banks, RRBs, and Cooperatives for rural credit, microfinance (SHG-Bank Linkage), Rural Infrastructure Development Fund (RIDF), and Farmer Producer Organizations (FPOs).\n- **SFAC (Small Farmers' Agribusiness Consortium):** Established in 1994 under the Ministry of Agriculture. Serves as the premier agency for mobilizing smallholders into FPOs, administering the **Equity Grant Fund** (matching equity up to \u20b915 Lakh per FPO) and the **Credit Guarantee Fund** (covering collateral-free loans up to \u20b92 Crore).\n- **APEDA (Agricultural and Processed Food Products Export Development Authority):** Established in 1986 under the Ministry of Commerce. Promotes export of scheduled agro-products (fruits, basmati rice, floriculture, processed items), financial assistance for cold chain infrastructure, quality certification, and international branding.\n- **District Industries Centres (DIC):** Initiated in 1978 to provide single-window support at the district level for MSME registration (Udyam), subsidy disbursement, industrial plots, and raw material access.\n- **MSME Development Act, 2006 (Revised 2020):** Classifies enterprises based on composite criteria of Investment in Plant & Machinery and Annual Turnover: Micro (<\u20b91 Cr / <\u20b95 Cr), Small (<\u20b910 Cr / <\u20b950 Cr), Medium (<\u20b950 Cr / <\u20b9250 Cr).",
