      "difficulty": "Easy",
      "syllabusTopic": "Meaning, definition, and concept of entrepreneur and entrepreneurship",
      "questionText": "Define the terms 'Entrepreneur', 'Entrepreneurship', and 'Enterprise'. How are these three concepts interrelated in the agribusiness sector?",
      "modelAnswer": "In economics and business management, the entrepreneurial phenomenon is structured around a three-dimensional framework: the actor, the action, and the institutional object.\n\n1. Entrepreneur (The Person / Actor):\nAn entrepreneur is an economic innovator, risk-taker, and visionary who identifies unexploited market opportunities, mobilizes scarce resources (land, labor, technology, capital), and orchestrates them into a productive venture. For instance, an agricultural graduate who spots high consumer demand for pesticide-free vegetables and decides to launch a protected-cultivation hydroponics venture is an entrepreneur.\n\n2. Entrepreneurship (The Process / Action):\nEntrepreneurship is the dynamic, creative, and systematic process through which the entrepreneur discovers opportunities, formulates business concepts, overcomes obstacles, accepts financial and reputational risks, and creates tangible value. In agriculture, this encompasses activities such as formulating soil-less nutrient recipes, establishing farm-to-table digital logistics, obtaining FSSAI certification, and negotiating off-take agreements with urban retailers.\n\n3. Enterprise (The Object / Outcome):\nAn enterprise is the resulting business entity, commercial organization, or legal firm established to deliver goods or services to the marketplace. It represents the physical, organizational, and financial vehicle\u2014such as 'GreenGrow Hydroponics Private Limited' or an accredited Farmer Producer Company (FPC).\n\nInterrelation in Agribusiness:\nThese three components operate as an organic continuum. The entrepreneur is the creative catalyst who drives the process of entrepreneurship, culminating in the establishment and growth of an enterprise. Without the proactive vision of the entrepreneur, resources remain dormant; without the execution process of entrepreneurship, ideas never materialize; and without the legal and operational structure of the enterprise, value cannot be sustained or scaled in competitive markets.",
      "outlinePoints": [
        "Definition of Entrepreneur as the individual actor and innovator",
        "Definition of Entrepreneurship as the creative, risk-bearing value creation process",
        "Definition of Enterprise as the organized commercial outcome/firm",
        "Illustration using a concrete agricultural example (hydroponics/FPC)",
        "Interconnection and systemic dependence of the triad"
      ]
