      "difficulty": "Easy",
      "syllabusTopic": "Institutional support: NABARD, SFAC, APEDA, MSME, and DIC roles",
      "questionText": "Explain the role and promotional schemes of the Small Farmers' Agribusiness Consortium (SFAC) in supporting Farmer Producer Organizations.",
      "modelAnswer": "The Small Farmers' Agribusiness Consortium (SFAC) was established in 1994 as an autonomous society under the Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare to promote private investment in agribusiness and organize small and marginal farmers into economically viable collectives.\n\nKey Roles and Flagship Schemes of SFAC:\n\n1. Mobilization and Incubation of Farmer Producer Organizations (FPOs):\nSFAC serves as the premier national nodal agency for forming, nurturing, and handholding Farmer Producer Companies (FPCs). It empanels Resource Institutions (RIs) to provide three years of cluster-level mobilization, legal registration, accounting support, and market linkage.\n\n2. Equity Grant Scheme (EGS):\nAccess to commercial credit requires a strong equity base. To enhance the net worth and borrowing capacity of FPCs, SFAC provides matching equity grants up to a maximum ceiling of \u20b915 Lakhs per FPC, matching the equity capital invested by farmer-members rupee for rupee (up to \u20b91,000 per member).\n\n3. Credit Guarantee Fund Scheme (CGFS):\nCommercial banks are traditionally hesitant to extend loans to newly registered FPOs lacking physical collateral. SFAC manages a dedicated Credit Guarantee Fund that provides institutional lenders guarantee cover for collateral-free credit facilities extended to FPCs up to \u20b92.00 Crore (recently extended up to \u20b95.00 Crore for qualifying entities).\n\n4. Venture Capital Assistance (VCA) Scheme:\nSFAC provides interest-free quasi-equity loans (Venture Capital Assistance) to individual agri-entrepreneurs and FPOs to bridge the promoter contribution margin mandated by banks for setting up agro-processing facilities.",
