      "syllabusTopic": "Institutional support: NABARD, SFAC, APEDA, MSME, and DIC roles",
      "questionText": "What are District Industries Centres (DICs)? Explain their primary support functions for rural micro-enterprises.",
      "modelAnswer": "District Industries Centres (DICs) were launched by the Government of India in May 1978 under the Industrial Policy Statement to provide an institutional focal point at the district headquarters level for the holistic promotion of micro, small, village, and cottage industries.\n\nKey Support Functions of DIC for Rural Micro-Enterprises:\n\n1. Single-Window Facilitation and Registration:\nDIC serves as the primary ground-level authority assisting entrepreneurs with Udyam registration, obtaining industrial approvals, electrical power sanctions, and clearance certificates from local authorities.\n\n2. Implementation of Government Self-Employment Schemes:\nDIC is the key nodal implementation agency for landmark subsidy schemes such as the Prime Minister\u2019s Employment Generation Programme (PMEGP). It scrutinizes applications, conducts task-force committee interviews, and sponsors viable project files to commercial banks for loan disbursement with capital subsidies (up to 35% in rural areas).\n\n3. Infrastructure and Industrial Land Allotment:\nDIC assists rural entrepreneurs in securing subsidized plots or built sheds within State Industrial Development Corporation (SIDC) parks and rural growth centers.\n\n4. Credit Linkage and Margin Money Assistance:\nIt facilitates credit appraisal with lead district banks and disburses state-specific fiscal incentives such as interest subsidies, power tariff subsidies, and quality certification reimbursements.\n\n5. Training and Raw Material Procurement:\nDIC conducts Entrepreneurship Development Programmes (EDPs) and assists rural artisans and agro-processors in securing controlled or scarce raw materials through state small-industries corporations.",
