      "difficulty": "Medium",
      "syllabusTopic": "Qualities, competencies, and entrepreneurial motivation (McClelland's theory)",
      "questionText": "Describe any five core entrepreneurial competencies identified by the Entrepreneurship Development Institute of India (EDII).",
      "modelAnswer": "An entrepreneurial competency is an underlying characteristic\u2014comprising knowledge, operational skills, and motive patterns\u2014that enables an individual to execute business roles with superior efficacy. The Entrepreneurship Development Institute of India (EDII), building on David McClelland's research, identified key behavioral competencies:\n\n1. Opportunity Seeking and Initiative:\nThe proactive ability to identify emerging consumer needs, gaps in local supply chains, or unutilized farm residues before competitors recognize them, and acting boldly without being compelled by external forces.\n\n2. Persistence:\nThe determination to take repeated, varied actions to overcome severe operational hurdles, crop failures, or regulatory bottlenecks. A persistent agri-entrepreneur does not abandon a custom hiring center because a tractor breaks down, but promptly establishes local repair partnerships.\n\n3. Systematic Planning and Monitoring:\nThe practice of deconstructing a broad commercial goal into time-phased, logical sub-tasks with allocated resources and continuous progress tracking against predetermined performance benchmarks.\n\n4. Demand for Efficiency and Quality:\nA personal passion to surpass standard benchmarks of excellence and complete activities faster, at lower cost, or with superior hygiene and chemical purity (such as obtaining ISO 22000 or organic standards).\n\n5. Information Seeking:\nDirectly engaging in deep, ongoing research rather than relying on hearsay. The entrepreneur consults scientists, visits wholesale mandis, questions progressive farmers, and analyzes government export data to guide enterprise decisions.",
