      "questionText": "Describe the mandate and functions of APEDA in facilitating agricultural export ventures from India.",
      "modelAnswer": "The Agricultural and Processed Food Products Export Development Authority (APEDA) is a statutory authority established by the Government of India under the APEDA Act passed by Parliament in December 1985. Operating under the administrative oversight of the Ministry of Commerce and Industry, APEDA serves as the apex trade promotion organization for agricultural commodities.\n\nKey Mandates and Functions of APEDA:\n\n1. Registration of Exporters:\nAny agribusiness enterprise exporting scheduled agricultural commodities\u2014such as basmati rice, mangoes, grapes, floriculture, processed vegetables, and buffalo meat\u2014must obtain mandatory registration with APEDA.\n\n2. Infrastructure and Cold-Chain Development:\nAPEDA provides financial assistance schemes to private agri-exporters and farmer groups for establishing post-harvest packhouses, vapor heat treatment (VHT) facilities, irradiation plants, pre-cooling units, and refrigerated transport trucks.\n\n3. Setting Quality and Sanitary Standards:\nAPEDA formulates export quality specifications, packaging guidelines, and sanitary/phytosanitary (SPS) compliance protocols. It operates traceability software platforms like 'GrapeNet', 'HortiNet', and 'MeatNet' that guarantee end-to-end residue-free traceability from the farm gate to the overseas consumer.\n\n4. Trade Promotion and Market Intelligence:\nIt organizes international trade fairs (e.g., AAHAR, Gulfood), sponsors buyer-seller meets, conducts overseas consumer market research, and secures market access agreements with foreign phytosanitary agencies.",
