      "difficulty": "Hard",
      "syllabusTopic": "Institutional support: NABARD, SFAC, APEDA, MSME, and DIC roles",
      "questionText": "Compare the organizational mandates, target beneficiaries, and operational mechanisms of NABARD and SFAC.",
      "modelAnswer": "While both NABARD and SFAC are premier institutions dedicated to rural and agricultural prosperity in India, their institutional scale, functional mandates, and operational instruments differ significantly:\n\n1. Institutional Genesis and Scale:\n- NABARD: Established in 1982 by an Act of Parliament as India's apex statutory development bank for agriculture and rural development, with a multi-billion-rupee balance sheet and offices in every state capital and district.\n- SFAC: Established in 1994 as an autonomous registered society under the Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare, operating as a specialized agency with a targeted mandate for smallholder aggregation.\n\n2. Core Functional Mandate:\n- NABARD: Focuses on macroeconomic rural finance, wholesale refinancing of cooperative banks and RRBs, monitoring rural credit policies, and funding public rural infrastructure projects (irrigation, roads, bridges via RIDF).\n- SFAC: Focuses specifically on mobilizing small and marginal farmers into Farmer Producer Organizations (FPOs/FPCs), facilitating agribusiness investments, and bridging the gap between smallholders and corporate supply chains.\n\n3. Target Beneficiaries:\n- NABARD: Broad institutional and rural spectrum\u2014Scheduled Commercial Banks, RRBs, State Cooperative Banks, State Governments, Panchayati Raj Institutions, SHGs, and individual rural borrowers through banking channels.\n- SFAC: Highly targeted\u2014Small and Marginal Farmers, Farmer Producer Companies (FPCs), and private agri-entrepreneurs setting up agro-processing facilities.\n\n4. Primary Operational Schemes and Financial Tools:\n- NABARD: Short-term and long-term refinancing facilities, Rural Infrastructure Development Fund (RIDF), Long-Term Irrigation Fund (LTIF), Micro Irrigation Fund (MIF), and direct loans to state entities.\n- SFAC: Matching Equity Grant Scheme (up to \u20b915 Lakhs per FPC), Credit Guarantee Fund Scheme (guaranteeing collateral-free bank loans up to \u20b92 Crore to FPCs), and Venture Capital Assistance (interest-free soft loans for agribusiness promoters).",
